<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :paint_maps %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:paint_maps) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Map slots') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every resolver takes an optional <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ el }</code>: the id
            or element whose nearest <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen</code> ancestor
            supplies the paint. Each returns a canonical
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">Fill</code>; see
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %> for the shape.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">slot(name, { el, kind })</code>: the Fill of one
              component slot. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">kind</code> is
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'bg'</code> (the default) for an area,
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'text'</code> for a label, or
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'border'</code> for a line, which returns a
              BorderFill whose <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">render.stroke</code> is the one
              color a line can take.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">series(i, count, { el })</code>: the fill for route
              <em>i</em> of <em>count</em>, from the same chart-series ramp charts use. See
              <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_charts) %>.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The area slots are <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-land</code> (the canvas),
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-water</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-green</code> (parks, forest, grass, wetland),
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-farmland</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-sand</code> (beach, sand, bare rock),
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-area</code> (residential, commercial, industrial),
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-site</code> (schools, hospitals, parking),
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-building</code> (also piers, dams, bridges) and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-transit</code> (station dots).
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The line slots are <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-road</code> (motorway to
            tertiary), <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-road-minor</code> (residential,
            service, pedestrian, runways), <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-path</code>
            (footway, cycleway, track, steps), <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-rail</code>
            (rail, tram, subway, aerialways), <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-boundary</code>
            and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-water-line</code> (rivers, canals, ferries).
            The text role <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map-label</code> inks every place and
            water label; tiers differ by size, not tone, because a small dithered label would not survive a busy map.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every map slot is a bg slot, lines included, so each resolves through the device mode: a dither tile
            on 1- and 2-bit screens, a solid on 4-bit and up, a hue on the color panels. A line painted from a tile
            is drawn as the polygon of its stroke filled with that tile, so a 1-bit road is the same screen-aligned
            dither its area would be, never a gray the panel cannot print; every line defaults to a gray short of
            the ink, so a plotted route in the ink reads on top of the roads.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Land follows the canvas and the labels follow the text channel, so a theme restates the token-bound
            areas and lines with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg-slot</code>, the mixin it
            uses for every other component: see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_slots) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// An area: a tile on 1-bit, a solid on 4-bit, blue on a color panel.
var water = TRMNLPaint.slot("map-water", { el: "my-map" });

// A line is a bg slot too: a tile on 1-bit (drawn as a line pattern), a gray on 4-bit.
var road = TRMNLPaint.slot("map-road", { el: "my-map" });

// A label: the ink of the map-label text role.
var label = TRMNLPaint.slot("map-label", { el: "my-map", kind: "text" });', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'MapLibre adapters') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Adapters shape a resolved <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">Fill</code> or
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">BorderFill</code> for one specific renderer. They copy
            resolved values into the renderer's native form; no adapter adds contrast heuristics, thresholds or
            substitute design rules.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">toMapLibre(fill)</code>: returns
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ color, ink, pattern }</code>. A solid Fill gives
              its color as both <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">color</code> and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">ink</code>; a tile Fill gives its painted ink and a
              registered pattern image for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-pattern</code>,
              with the field composited in and a pixel ratio that lands one tile pixel on one device pixel; a line
              Fill gives its stroke and never a pattern.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLMaps</code> (see the next section) is the
            MapLibre composition layer over TRMNLPaint, the way
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code> is for Highcharts:
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_charts) %>. Pattern images are added to the map as the style asks
            for them, so a plugin never handles the image itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// MapLibre: a flat color on solid panels, a registered pattern on 1- and 2-bit screens.
var water = TRMNLPaint.toMapLibre(TRMNLPaint.slot("map-water", { el: "my-map" }));
var paint = water.pattern
  ? { "fill-pattern": water.pattern.id }   // dither tile
  : { "fill-color": water.color };         // solid

// A line layer: TRMNLMaps.style() widens a tile line into a fill with the tile after the
// tiles load; by hand, a solid rail gives you the color and a dashed line takes the ink.
var road = TRMNLPaint.toMapLibre(TRMNLPaint.slot("map-road", { el: "my-map" }));
var linePaint = { "line-color": road.color || road.ink, "line-width": TRMNLPaint.px(2, { el: "my-map" }) };', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The TRMNLMaps API') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLMaps</code> ships in the same
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">plugins.js</code> runtime as
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code> and composes MapLibre GL JS styles
            and options out of it. It resolves no paint of its own: every value it returns comes from a TRMNLPaint
            resolver and adapter. <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :map) %> puts it to work in street, route and Strava
            examples.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every method takes the same optional <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ el }</code> as
            the resolvers: the map container id or element whose nearest
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.screen</code> supplies the paint. Omit it on a
            single-screen plugin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Resolving map paint') %>
            <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">paint(token, { el })</code>: one palette token as
                MapLibre paint, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ color, ink, pattern }</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">series(i, count, { el })</code>: the MapLibre paint
                for route <em>i</em> of <em>count</em> from the screen's chart-series ramp.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">path(i, count, { el, width })</code>: a line layer's
                layout and paint for that route, rounded caps and joins,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">width</code> scaled through
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.px()</code> (default 2).
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">marker(i, count, { el, radius, hollow })</code>: a
                circle layer's paint for that route, the series ink with a contrast ring (default radius 4).
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">hollow: true</code> swaps ring and fill, so a start
                dot and an end ring read apart on one ink.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">applySwatches({ el })</code>: paints every element
                under the screen carrying <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">data-map-slot="map-water"</code>
                (and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">data-map-slot-kind="text"</code> or
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">"border"</code>) from that slot, for a legend.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Building the style') %>
            <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">tiles(preset)</code>: a tile source preset, the
                vector tile URL, zoom range and attribution.
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'osm'</code> is the default; an object merges over it
                for a custom host.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">style(preset, { el, tiles, labels, buildings })</code>:
                a complete MapLibre style for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">streets</code>,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">minimal</code>,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">outline</code> or
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">blank</code>, every layer painted from the map
                slots. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">labels: false</code> drops the labels,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">labels: 'major'</code> keeps the big place names,
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">buildings: false</code> drops the footprints.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">options({ el, preset, center, zoom })</code>: Map
                options for a still map: the container, every handler and animation off, no controls, the screen's
                pixel ratio, and the style for the preset. Pass the result to
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">new maplibregl.Map()</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">merge(base, overrides)</code>: a deep merge of two
                plain objects, where arrays and scalars replace. Layer your own ids, sources and options over the
                defaults with it.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Fitting and decoding') %>
            <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fit(map, coords, { padding, maxZoom })</code>: frames
                the coordinates without animation, on an integer zoom with the center snapped to the pixel grid so
                dither patterns stay crisp. Returns the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">{ center, zoom }</code>
                it jumped to.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">decodePolyline(str, precision)</code>: a Google
                encoded polyline (Strava's <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">map.summary_polyline</code>)
                as <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">[lng, lat]</code> pairs, ready for a GeoJSON
                LineString.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Keeping the map current') %>
            <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch(el, buildFn)</code>: builds the map now and
                again whenever the device, scale, mode, dark-mode or theme classes change.
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">buildFn</code> creates and returns the map; the
                previous one is removed first and the new one attached. Returns a stop function.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">attach(map, { el })</code>: registers a map you
                built yourself: pattern images, the pixel-grid snap, the labels, the attribution and readiness.
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch()</code> calls it for you.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">ready(map)</code>: a promise that resolves once the
                map has drawn everything it knows about.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">settle({ maxWaitMs })</code>: the bounded wait the
                runtime runs at the end of a pass, so
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">window.TRMNL_PLUGINS_READY</code> flips only once
                every attached map is idle. Default 6000 ms, or
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">window.__TRMNL_MAPS_SETTLE_MS__</code>.
              </li>
              <li>
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">supported()</code>: whether this browser can draw a
                MapLibre map. Without WebGL, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">watch()</code> flags the
                container <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">data-map-unsupported</code> and shows a
                <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">.map__fallback</code> child if you placed one.
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">
              MapLibre numbers do not read CSS, so resolve widths, radii and padding with
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.px()</code> inside the build function. See
              <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="space-y-2">
            <%= framework_heading(4, 'Labels') %>
            <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">
              Place and water labels are framework elements, not MapLibre text. After every idle the runtime reads
              the label features out of the loaded tiles, writes each one into the container as a
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label</code> (big places) or
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label label--small</code> (towns, suburbs, water)
              with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke text-stroke--large</code>, snaps it
              to whole pixels, and keeps the biggest that fit without overlap. Every tier takes the map-label ink.
            </p>
            <p class="<%= framework_subsection_description_classes %>">
              So labels take the screen's own typography (TRMNL pixel fonts on 1-bit and 2-bit low-density panels,
              Inter on 4-bit and high density), the text-stroke halo, and the map-label slot for ink, with no glyph
              endpoint involved. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">style()</code> decides which
              tiers a preset shows.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: 'var el = "my-map";

// watch() rebuilds on every device, mode, dark-mode and theme change.
TRMNLMaps.watch(el, function () {
  var map = new maplibregl.Map(TRMNLMaps.options({ el: el, preset: "minimal" }));
  map.on("load", function () {
    map.addSource("route", { type: "geojson", data: routeFeature });
    map.addLayer(TRMNLMaps.merge(TRMNLMaps.path(0, 1, { el: el, width: 3 }), { id: "route", source: "route" }));
  });
  TRMNLMaps.fit(map, coords, { padding: TRMNLPaint.px(20, { el: el }), maxZoom: 15 });
  return map;
});', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Live example') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The swatches below are painted with
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLMaps.applySwatches()</code> inside
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.watch()</code>, with no map library
            involved. Change the device mode or Style in the screen picker and they repaint from the live cascade:
            tiles on 1- and 2-bit screens, solids on 4-bit, hues on a color panel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="<%= framework_example_classes %>">
          <div class="<%= framework_example_inner_classes %>">
            <div class="<%= framework_example_wrapper_classes %>">
              <div class="screen">
                <div class="view view--full">
                  <div class="layout layout--col gap--space-between">
                    <div class="grid grid--cols-3" id="map-slot-swatches">
                      <div class="item">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <div class="w--14 h--4 mb--2 rounded--small" data-map-slot="map-land"></div>
                          <span class="label">Land</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="item">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <div class="w--14 h--4 mb--2 rounded--small" data-map-slot="map-water"></div>
                          <span class="label">Water</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="item">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <div class="w--14 h--4 mb--2 rounded--small" data-map-slot="map-green"></div>
                          <span class="label">Green</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="item">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <div class="w--14 h--4 mb--2 rounded--small" data-map-slot="map-building"></div>
                          <span class="label">Building</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="item">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <div class="w--14 h--1.5 mb--2" data-map-slot="map-road"></div>
                          <span class="label">Road</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                      <div class="item">
                        <div class="meta"></div>
                        <div class="content">
                          <div class="w--14 h--1.5 mb--2" data-map-slot="map-road-minor"></div>
                          <span class="label">Minor road</span>
                        </div>
                      </div>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="title_bar">
                    <img class="image image--adaptive" src="/images/plugins/trmnl--render.svg" alt="TRMNL Logo">
                    <span class="title">Painting Maps</span>
                    <span class="instance">Map slots</span>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <script type="text/trmnl-example">
                  function whenReady(cb) {
                    var tries = 0;
                    (function attempt() {
                      if (window.TRMNLMaps) return cb();
                      if (++tries > 200) return;
                      setTimeout(attempt, 50);
                    })();
                  }

                  whenReady(function () {
                    var el = "map-slot-swatches";
                    TRMNLPaint.watch(el, function () {
                      TRMNLMaps.applySwatches({ el: el });
                    });
                  });
                </script>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '<div class="w--14 h--4 mb--2 rounded--small" data-map-slot="map-water"></div>
<div class="w--14 h--1.5 mb--2" data-map-slot="map-road"></div>

<script type="text/javascript">
  var el = "map-slot-swatches";
  TRMNLPaint.watch(el, function () {
    TRMNLMaps.applySwatches({ el: el });
  });
</script>') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
